# Terraform AWS Lightsail Apache Deployment

## Overview

This project uses **Terraform** to provision an **AWS Lightsail Ubuntu server** and configure an Apache web server using Terraform `user_data`.

The server automatically:

* Creates an AWS Lightsail instance
* Uses Ubuntu 24.04
* Installs Apache2
* Starts and enables Apache2
* Creates a custom HTML web page
* Outputs important server information such as the public IP address

## Architecture

```text
Terraform
    |
    v
AWS Lightsail
    |
    v
Ubuntu 24.04
    |
    v
Apache2
    |
    v
Custom Web Page
```

## Prerequisites

Before running this project, make sure you have:

* AWS account
* AWS CLI installed
* Terraform installed
* AWS credentials configured
* Git installed

Verify Terraform:

```bash
terraform version
```

Verify AWS CLI:

```bash
aws --version
```

Check your AWS credentials:

```bash
aws sts get-caller-identity
```

## Project Structure

```text
Terraform-Lab/
│
├── main.tf
├── outputs.tf
├── variables.tf
├── .gitignore
└── README.md
```

## Terraform Configuration

Example `main.tf`:

```hcl
resource "aws_lightsail_instance" "Test-Sever1" {
  name              = "Server1"
  availability_zone = "us-east-1a"
  blueprint_id      = "ubuntu_24_04"
  bundle_id         = "nano_3_0"

  user_data = <<-EOF
    #!/bin/bash
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y apache2
    systemctl start apache2
    systemctl enable apache2
    echo '<h1>This is deployed by Safiatu Seidu</h1>' > /var/www/html/index.html
  EOF
}
```

## Initialize Terraform

Initialize the Terraform working directory:

```bash
terraform init
```

## Format the Code

```bash
terraform fmt
```

## Validate the Configuration

```bash
terraform validate
```

A successful validation should return:

```text
Success! The configuration is valid.
```

## Review the Deployment Plan

```bash
terraform plan
```

Review the resources Terraform plans to create before proceeding.

## Deploy the Infrastructure

Apply the Terraform configuration:

```bash
terraform apply
```

Or automatically approve:

```bash
terraform apply --auto-approve
```

## Terraform Outputs

Example `outputs.tf`:

```hcl
output "availability_zone" {
  value = aws_lightsail_instance.Test-Sever1.availability_zone
}

output "cpu_count" {
  value = aws_lightsail_instance.Test-Sever1.cpu_count
}

output "private_ip_address" {
  value = aws_lightsail_instance.Test-Sever1.private_ip_address
}

output "public_ip_address" {
  value = aws_lightsail_instance.Test-Sever1.public_ip_address
}

output "key_pair_name" {
  value = aws_lightsail_instance.Test-Sever1.key_pair_name
}
```

Display the outputs:

```bash
terraform output
```

To display only the public IP:

```bash
terraform output public_ip_address
```

## Verify Apache

SSH into the Lightsail instance and check Apache:

```bash
systemctl status apache2
```

Check the Apache process:

```bash
ps aux | grep '[a]pache2'
```

Check whether Apache is listening on port 80:

```bash
ss -tulpn | grep :80
```

Test Apache locally:

```bash
curl http://localhost
```

Expected output:

```html
<h1>This is deployed by Safiatu Seidu</h1>
```

## Access the Website

Get the public IP:

```bash
terraform output public_ip_address
```

Open the following in a browser:

```text
http://PUBLIC_IP_ADDRESS
```

The page should display:

```text
This is deployed by Safiatu Seidu
```

## Troubleshooting

### Apache is not installed

Check:

```bash
apache2 -v
```

Install Apache manually if necessary:

```bash
apt update
apt install -y apache2
systemctl enable --now apache2
```

### Apache is not running

Check:

```bash
systemctl status apache2
```

Start it:

```bash
systemctl start apache2
```

### Check Apache logs

```bash
journalctl -u apache2 --no-pager
```

Apache error log:

```bash
cat /var/log/apache2/error.log
```

### Website is not accessible

Check whether port 80 is listening:

```bash
ss -tulpn | grep :80
```

Also verify that **TCP port 80 is allowed in the AWS Lightsail firewall**.

## Destroy the Infrastructure

When you are finished with the lab:

```bash
terraform destroy
```

Automatically approve:

```bash
terraform destroy --auto-approve
```

**Warning:** This permanently removes the Terraform-managed Lightsail resources.

